  {
    "episode_id": 72778,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72778.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Can you help to explain some of the goals and objectives of the Senate Broadband Caucus that you're a part of?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Senate Broadband Caucus has specific goals and objectives.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "I am a member of the Senate Broadband Caucus.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The person I'm speaking to possesses knowledge about the Senate Broadband Caucus.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "It is reasonable to expect that someone who is a member would understand the caucus's goals and objectives.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Explaining the goals and objectives of the caucus will contribute to meaningful dialogue on broadband policy.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener values transparency and clarity regarding legislative initiatives.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72778,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72778.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Yes. This is something that's a bipartisan caucus with Shelley Capito, the Republican of West Virginia, and Angus King, the Independent of Maine Center High Camp and myself, North Dakota and Minnesota, and then the Center of Bothmen of Arkansas. And we came together because we're still just not at all satisfied with the status quo, that 39% of Americans in rural America lack access to broadband. You know, sure it used to be, can they get dial up and have some access? Well, some of them can, but it's not the kind of high speed access that you need to farm or to run a small business. And I believe that kids that grow up in rural America should be able to live in rural America. And that means getting the kind of access that we need.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Currently, 39% of Americans in rural areas lack access to broadband internet.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a bipartisan congressional caucus involving Shelley Moore Capito, Angus King, and the speaker.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Shelley Moore Capito represents West Virginia as a Republican.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Angus King represents Maine as an Independent.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The group formed due to dissatisfaction with the current state of rural broadband access.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Dial-up internet is available to some rural residents but does not constitute adequate high-speed connectivity.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker represents North Dakota and Minnesota.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "High-speed internet is necessary for modern farming operations.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "High-speed internet is essential for running small businesses in rural areas.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The caucus includes members from both Arkansas and the Center of Bothmen of Arkansas.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Bipartisan cooperation on infrastructure issues is possible and effective.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Rural communities deserve equitable technological infrastructure comparable to urban areas.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Access to high-speed internet is a prerequisite for economic opportunity and quality of life in rural regions.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that people should be able to thrive in their home communities without needing to relocate to cities.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "High-speed broadband is a fundamental necessity rather than a luxury for rural development.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Current policy efforts have failed to resolve rural broadband inequity despite prior investments.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s political identity carries legitimacy and influence in shaping national broadband policy.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Audience members value rural equity and recognize the importance of digital access.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Technological advancement in rural areas supports long-term demographic sustainability.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The term 'Center of Bothmen' refers to a legitimate political or organizational entity within Arkansas.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72778,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72778.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Is rural broadband now considered still a luxury? Or is this more of an essential service, much like telecommunications and even electricity from years ago?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Rural broadband is currently being debated as either a luxury or an essential service.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The comparison between rural broadband and historical utilities like electricity and telecommunications is being made.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "There is a societal consensus on what constitutes an 'essential service' that should be universally accessible.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Historical precedents like electricity and telecommunications were once considered luxuries before becoming recognized as essentials.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Broadband access has similar transformative potential for modern life as past utilities did.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Government or public institutions have a responsibility to ensure universal access to essential services.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72778,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72778.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's becoming more and more essential when you have farmers that are having to drive into town and do business in the myself parking lot or You know, companies like Genio that actually built fiber to farms so that they could measure the temperature of the barn with the avian flu epidemic that we had last year. That's not doable for all of rural America. And I think it's much better if we just had actual broadband to the farm and to the towns. And so things have changed and business has changed. And that's what I'm so struck by 10 years ago. People were just talking about getting any access. And now they want to have the kind of access that you have in the urban areas, and they should get it. And that's why we're looking at things like the universal service fund, which is a huge amount of money set aside that people pay on their phone bill, which goes to things like underserved areas, like rural and then low income areas, and taking a chunk of that money and investing that in broadband. Senator Soon. the head of the Commerce Committee in which I serve and I joined together. We got over 60 senators on a letter to the FCC saying let's start doing more on the universal fund and they actually responded and have put some money out there. I think that the more we can do in finding creative ways to fund rural broadband, that's what this pocket is about.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Universal Service Fund collects contributions through phone bills to support telecommunications in underserved regions.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "It is increasingly essential to have reliable internet connectivity in rural areas.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Over 60 U.S. Senators signed a joint letter urging the FCC to increase funding for broadband via the Universal Service Fund.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The current level of internet access in rural America is insufficient for modern agricultural needs.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Universal broadband access comparable to urban areas is both desirable and achievable for rural communities.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Farmers currently need to travel to town to conduct business due to inadequate local infrastructure.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The FCC has responded positively and released funds toward broadband expansion based on senatorial advocacy.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Ten years ago, rural stakeholders primarily sought basic internet access rather than high-speed connectivity.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Companies like Genio have deployed fiber-optic networks directly to farms to support monitoring during health crises.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "A significant portion of the Universal Service Fund is allocated to rural and low-income areas.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
