  {
    "episode_id": 72778,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72778.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Yes. You look at the fact that now you want to have healthcare and rural communities, but you want to get them the best available expertise. One of the ways with increasingly complicated, complex medical availability on the internet, you're in procedures and you can consult with a doctor at the Mayo Clinic. When you're in a small town in Worthington, Minnesota, you can consult with someone. And so what we want to be able to do is use more and more broadband access so that doctors can do everything from scheduling to getting medications to checking medication list to be able to actually consult with experts, and the more we go with this, they could be consulting with them in the middle of a surgery. In that way, rural patients are going to get the best access to healthcare and all of that requires high-speed access.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Rural communities need access to the best available medical expertise.",
