    "turn_text": "all these other ports have American computer systems. But they're not allowed to do that. And I just don't think that's good for the people, and it's not good for ad country, and it's not good for America. And so our hope is that by lifting the whole embargo, we would be in the best place in terms of doing business. One interesting agriculture perspective, having been there now with Secretary Bill Seck with the President and meeting with that group there, is there's a real opportunity for organic agriculture in Cuba because, and I know some of our domestic providers sometimes concerned about the competition. Well, as you know, we have a big organic market here and we can make about 1% of it, about 20% of what we need, about 5% of the needs are organic right now. And so there's a big market there for organic produce where they could split scale, not just little farms with big scale, start doing organic because of the land quality and all kinds of things and get certified right away. So that's one of the things that Bill Beck is pushing for, and I don't think that would directly be competitive nearly as much because we need more organic. So that was an interesting thing that I learned over there. The other one, and I'll end with this on Cuba, is just be it. amazing change that we've seen in the attitude of the American people and the Cuban people with us on the plane last summer where we opened the embassy with Secretary Kerry with three Marines. Mike Berry and I think the other guy's name was Joe or something like that. And they had come with us because they were the ones that were given a really difficult job 50 some years ago of taking the American flag down over the embassy when we closed it. to jeering crowds, and one of them turned to the crowds and said, we will return. 50 some years later, the old Marines were back and they handed a new flag to three young Marines who hoisted up over the embassy. And it just gives you that sense that things c