    "turn_text": "you know, come on now, they're open all months and they're a really big state. And this bear is located in the middle of the Twin Cities. And I have always found that most people would say, well, that's hard to get to for a farmer. But what happens is all the farmers come, they see all the city people, and all the city people remember where the food came from. They see the biggest hogs. They are able to go to machinery. They are able to see how honey's made. And they're able to see crop art. And it just brings these two worlds together. And I think that is one of our goals as a member of the Agriculture Committee working with Colin Peterson, my friend from Minnesota, the ranking in the House Committee. It's really make that case for the how agriculture and the rural economy are the backbone of our country. And whether we need making sure as we go forward that we get the Farm Bill Reopt, we know there's other issues facing rural America too. And that's broadband access as we talked about today. That is making sure that we have housing. That's become a huge crisis that the economy has improved and trying to find innovative ways to get money for housing. And the third thing that I don't think anyone expected five years ago was this opioid addiction and the epidemic spreading across rural America. So we just passed a bipartisan bill on that. I'm hoping to get more money for treatment and we're really working less because these are unique challenges in rural areas that we didn't have maybe 10, 20 years ago that need to be fixed. I'm looking forward to going to visit with a number of our farming communities and rural areas in August. I visit all 87 counties every single year in some of the counties with one small count. I think I know everyone in it. But it's a great part of my job and I'm honored to be on the Agriculture Committee.",
